iving thought  to questions you sent some my way that  were really great ones so i look forward  to the conversation with all of you  so a little bit more about my  perspective  i started a firm called green order in  2000  that worked with large companies and  works with large companies today  to help make energy and environmental  innovation a source of competitive  advantage and we've worked with a really  broad array of industries and corporate  leaders as stuart said  there's just a couple of examples here  on this slide but more than 100  companies across diverse industries  we were very fortunate to start working  with general electric in 2004  help them to create their business  strategy around clean technology called  eco-imagination some of you have  probably seen the marketing but  at its heart eco imagination was a  business strategy to invest in clean  technology and innovation around energy  and environment and so  i'll come back to the work that we've  done with ge it's really central to  where i've gone in my career over the  last seven or eight years  i also have had the occasion to work on  some other  fun and interesting projects we worked  with general motors on the launch of the  chevrolet volt  which was really a pioneering electric  vehicle  worked with silverstein properties in  new york on the creation of seven world  trade center the first building that  went up at ground zero which was the  first leed certified office tower in new  york city  and we worked with leading energy  companies like duke energy and national  grid and others  so i've had the benefit of working  across a variety of different sectors  and seeing how large corporates think  about  these issues  and the reason i'm excited  about this space is while there are  great challenges ahead of us when it  comes to energy security and  environmental issues and climate change  there's also great opportunity  uh and that opportunity i see is the  opportunity to solve our resource  challenges using innovation if we look  at the fundamentals and those of you who  are here in the school of forestry and  environmental studies you you know all  this uh well but  there are some key drivers that i think  make this an exciting area to focus on  for the next  generations to come 20 30 40 years first  the sheer fact that our population is  growing at such a a huge clip  towards 10 billion people globally  the fact that three billion people will  join the middle class by 2030.  uh tom friedman of the new york times  likes to say that people all over the  world are going to want to live like  americans and while that's a great thing  in some ways it also means that the  pressure and constraints on our natural  resources are going to be huge  and that means that we must figure out  new ways to make energy water  forestry other resources abundant  cleaner and more efficient and so i  think that is really the task of the  21st century and it's why i'm doing this  work and i'm sure why so many of you are  as well  we need to innovate in how we produce  and create  essentially everything in our society  and that leads me to this kind of big  metaphysical question if you will which  is what do we mean when we talk about  innovation we use that term so much  right and i think it's worth stepping  back and kind of asking ourselves what  is innovation really all about  because it's easy to kind of quickly say  well innovation that's about technology  but it's about more than technology it's  also about culture  and it's more than just great big ideas  as i'll talk about further it's also  about having the courage to take action  and to actually be willing to fail and  make mistakes  so let's explore a little bit about how  both big corporate actors and others are  thinking about innovation  so leading companies are trying to  innovate to solve problems they see  market opportunities they see an ability  to bring new products and services to  market  they also want to improve their own  performance right they know that if they  can operate more efficiently they can  produce greater profitability more  enterprise value  they want to create products and  services that the market wants and by  innovating they hope to do that and  ultimately increase market share and get  price premiums and loyalty in the market  and they want better engagement with the  marketplace that means with public  stakeholders  but it also means  accessing innovation no matter where it  may be  and so companies like general electric  and others are embracing this concept of  open innovation and even though they  have huge resources and investments in  their own r d  general electric for example across  their global research centers has 3 000  scientists who do nothing but look at  new technologies and applications and  that's outside of their business units  where they have thousands of more  technologists and r d folks looking at  new ideas  so open innovation is this concept of  looking outside the four walls of the  enterprise whether it's investing in  university research or looking at  investing in new startups or working  collaboratively with others in the  marketplace  and this is a big economic opportunity  mckinsey put out a study last year  saying that over the next 20 years there  is three trillion dollars in economic  benefit by improving resource efficiency  in the global marketplace so there's a  lot at stake here and a lot of reason to  focus for big companies on this area  these are big markets  at the same time you've got some of the  most talented entrepreneurs and  investors who have gravitated into the  energy and resources area  because they see the same economic  opportunity and because they're driven  by ideals and values and they want to  make a difference in the world and  that's incredibly exciting to me  i started in this space in 2000  sort of at the height of the dot-com  bubble  and there were not that many people at  that