stainable  transportation  we're coming up with new products and  services but they're not scaling up fast  enough they're not commercializing  they're not reaching a mass audience  so i ask all of you i mean think about  how many new companies or products you  can think of that over the last five or  seven or even 10 years have gone  completely mainstream in this space and  you can probably count them on one hand  when we get to the q and a maybe we can  talk about some because i'd love to hear  if you can name you know more than five  most people can't  so why is that i mean first off and here  we are in the academy we have to ask  ourselves  are there failures of public policy and  i think most of us would say yes both on  the international stage i think the un  framework while  uh you know a good attempt has not  gotten us where we want to go in terms  of action on climate change on the  national stage in the united states we  also have not made the progress in terms  of energy and climate policy even though  there have been some good attempts and  some good efforts in specific areas like  fuel economy  at the state and regional level there  are some more reasons to be i think  pleased with the achievements that we've  made and particularly i'm excited about  some of the efforts that cities have  made and regions have made whether you  look at the new england region or  california  but there's also realities around  economics right most people know that  with natural gas prices where they are  two three dollars a million btu it's  very hard for alternative technologies  in energy and transportation  even  impacting the infrastructure space to  compete and so that's one of the  realities of why a lot of these  solutions haven't scaled up that fast  and then  i always like to say think about it you  know two guys in a garage  cannot disrupt the power sector right  here we are we're in the general motors  room i don't think gm is concerned about  two guys in a garage coming up with a  vehicle that's going to completely upend  gm now gm has had other challenges over  the recent years but they're coming back  strong post bankruptcy  but if you think about our whole  conception of startups and disruptive  innovation right the whole history of it  is really  about the renegade entrepreneur think  about hewlett and packard who created  the whole personal computer revolution  at the start or steve jobs and steve  wozniak with apple or bill gates uh and  his colleagues at microsoft or later  larry and sergey at google  or mark zuckerberg at facebook these  were all examples of literally in most  of those in cases these were guys in a  garage not gals but guys but there are  certainly stories of gals doing it too  who are creating new products and  solutions that upended industries and  created multi-billion dollar enterprises  and we don't see that in the energy  sector we don't see that in  transportation and water and waste and  other sectors that are relevant to the  issues we're talking about today  this has led a lot of the analysts in  the sector to talk about this idea of a  valley of death right and it's not just  a technology valley of death where  you're working to perfect  a new solution or a new  technology application it's a  commercialization value of death which  is essentially a fancy way of saying  that even if you can create the widget  that will change the world if you can't  figure out how to get it to market how  to make it economical on a per unit  basis it's very hard to compete with the  existing solutions and that means for  many new clean technology  products and solutions that have been  created it's very hard for them to go up  against the incumbent technologies  so  all of this has led me over recent years  to think we really need to step back and  ask ourselves what are we talking about  when we talk about innovation because we  get excited about new technologies and  new ideas  but the truth is if we think about  innovation and what it really means in  terms of its impact  innovation is about more than ideas  innovation has to be innovation excuse  me innovation has to be ideas  as applied  and implemented at scale  i mean it's a little bit to me like the  story about if a tree falls in the  forest right  that old kind of cohen if a tree falls  in the forest and nobody hears it  has it actually happened  that's the same thing with innovation if  you come up with a brilliant idea  but it is not implemented at scale  that is an innovation that's simply a  concept or an idea or something that's  on the drawing board which has yet to be  applied and to change the world to make  an impact whether it's environmentally  financially  from the other perspectives that we  might take national security public  health  we have to take these ideas and apply  them and implement them at scale in  order to really make a difference  so let me then step back and tell you  how we're trying to work on these issues  at broad scale  as we look at the marketplace of these  new ideas if you think about it there  are a lot of fantastic new technologies  and companies that have been launched  over the last five to seven years and in  fact it's been a fertile period as i  said there's been more venture capital  going into this  space than ever before there's been more  project finance capital  corporates are more involved so you have  great companies that are being created  great ideas that are  coming into the market  but the truth is very few of them are  getting through this funnel of  commercialization to the end  they may be raising capital moving  forward in their commercial plan and  getting to growth stage but then they  get stuck in this valley of death as we  discussed before  so what's the best way to actually drive  impact and we've been thinking about it  and we've come up with what we think is  a new approach  let me actually before i get there pause  with a quick historical side note  here again since we're at yale i can't  resist but telling you a little story  and this is actually from a book  written by my friend stephen johnson  the book is called future perfect  stephen writes about innovation and  science his last book was called where  good ideas come from a natural history  of innovation  and he looked at what were the best  mechanisms to drive progress in society  and basically because he's a theorist  about technology and innovation he  looked historical examples and he came  up with this interesting example this is  the french railroad system that was  created in the mid 19th century  by a  designer named victor legrand who was  commissioned by the french government to  create a rail system this was of course  the cutting edge technology at the time  and he created what can only be thought  to be an incredibly  elegant  system and design for a national  railroad  highly centralized  very top-down  beautifully architected  but at the end of the day  not that efficient  and the place where that became clearest  was during the franco-prussian war in  1870 when the french and german went to  war  on this eastern front of france  and the germans had a huge advantage  because they had rail lines they were  far more decentralized  there were  multiple redundant lines to get german  soldiers to the front  and they were able to use those rail  lines to get  troops and munitions there faster than  the french could and most military  historians chalk up the german victory  in that war partly to the difference in  the architecture of their respective  rail networks  now we may have historians in the room  who know better than i but what i find  fascinating about this story was that  the best laid plans of the french in  coming up with a perfect system for a  rail architecture  were upended by something that wasn't  created top down that wasn't by design  centralized and was in fact quite  hodgepodge  and stephen johnson says we need to  learn from this and look at other  systems that can actually be much more  efficient in terms of scaling up  impact and ideas  the alternative he says is think about  the value of distributed decentralized  networks and he actually fast forwards  from the mid 1800s excuse me yeah the  mid-1800s into the mid-1900s 100 almost  100 years later  to the work of a communications theorist  named paul buran  now paul buran was a researcher at the  rand corporation  and he was given a complex problem to  solve which was how do we create  a communications infrastructure that can  survive a nuclear  bomb  this was just at the time when nuclear  weaponry  was  being used for the first time and the  real scare was  based on the centrality of the  communications network could the entire  network be taken down because of single  points of vulnerability  and what beran came up with was rather  than a centralized network as you see on  the left or even a decentralized network  in the middle with multiple nodes  was  the value of having a highly distributed  network such as the one that you see on  the right where there would be no  central node or even not that many  decentralized nodes but multiple nodes  and redundancies now those of you who  have spent any time studying the history  of the internet  know that brands work and this  distributed network went on to influence  and ultimately inform the very structure  that created the internet and so when  people have talked about the internet  was created to withstand you know a  nuclear bomb that was brand's work at  rand which ultimately impacted  communications and network theory and  led to the creations of all the  protocols that underlie the internet  today  so i'm taking that historical detour  because i want to talk for a moment  about our theory on how to create and  scale up innovation and how to do so in  a way that will be most impactful and  most efficient and essentially  we are drawing from this network theory  to say  that we need another approach to think  about how we take those great ideas in  the energy and resource space  and scale them up at impact and that  approach is one of relying on the power  of networks  so rather than the historical model  where  companies have been funded and grown and  then gone out to market to find  customers wherever they might be  we are trying to create a structure  that's network-based bringing in a group  of corporations  who can be both customers for those new  technologies and innovations  as well as sponsors in terms of  providing capital to those companies as  well as partners in terms of  commercialization distribution and other  sources of value manufacturing and  licensing partnerships financing  and  this is interesting  outlook uh  really key partners in scaling up  these new innovations and technologies  that are being brought to market  so we are borrowing from network theory  to try to find  a new way to accelerate  the creation of clean energy  technologies and ideas that can change  the world  we're starting in the power sector as i  think i said earlier with  representation from ge  duke energy the largest utility in the  united states and national grid which is  the largest  power company in the united kingdom but  also owns utility assets here in the  northeast of the u.s  but our aspiration over time is to go  into adjacent areas so areas like oil  and gas and transportation and real  estate and infrastructure and water  and waste and food and agriculture  and materials  and the reasoning there is that these  clusters  while distinct industry areas are  increasingly overlapping and the power  will be in pulling together different  companies across these industry areas  into the kind of robust distributed  network  that bran wrote about and ultimately  helped influence the creation of our  contemporary internet-based economy  and so what we're hoping to do over time  is create  a distributed network of large  corporates who can help take these ideas  and take them to scale  so our approach is what we call the  formation of creative networks of  collaboration  and this is a phrase that actually i  would say we to some degree borrowed  from president bill clinton who's been  talking about the power of collaboration  and networks as well  we added the idea of trying to make them  more creative networks because we think  that we need to go beyond traditional  modes of collaboration and partnership  and by that  what do we mean  well the first thing is  we're not just interested in putting  capital to work in companies what we're  trying to facilitate here  is  yes capital but also commercialization  partnerships that can help accelerate  these technologies overcome that valley  of death that we talked about earlier if  we don't do that we know that a lot of  these companies even with a lot of  capital  may not succeed there are just too many  difficult  uh challenges in the process of scaling  up these businesses of finding the  channels to market of having the  confidence of the end users in the  market  to go it alone  we're also looking to try to aggregate  demand among different players in our  network so if we can pull together  multiple companies that can share the  risk we can actually help technologies  to get to market faster  in terms of network theory the benefits  to our members include greater  efficiency in terms of transaction costs  and information costs they can share the  burden of evaluating new companies and  technologies and they can also begin to  share information amongst one another  in ways that will give them greater  insight and intelligence into the market  and these new  technologies and then lastly the kind of  collaborative model we're talking about  is one in which both  the large corporate network members and  the startup companies that we're working  with benefit both sides get something  out of the equation  and the best news is that there are  positive