acts and growing  the number of customers that recycle  bank has had and so it's a good example  of the kind of  good outcome you can come you can get  when you put together a partnership like  this  another example i wanted to mention is  silver spring networks silver spring is  a smart grid uh technology company which  is working with utilities  around the world it's been a leader in  this space  and interestingly just over the last  year or so they've forged two  partnerships with large corporations  both of those partnerships involved  equity investment again but they didn't  stop there they also involve joint r d  and development of solutions and  products to the market together so with  hitachi they are entering the  international market and particularly  japan  and with emc which is a company that  focuses on the data space they're  developing new solutions around big data  to analyze uh utility information and  make it more valuable to their end  customers so it's another interesting  example of of how  uh  two large incumbent players are working  with a younger company that's a leader  in the space  and then the third one i'll mention is  tesla and we talked briefly i mentioned  elon musk before but when i speak to  audiences i often ask them you know  what's the most successful new  energy or resource company or clean tech  company if you want to call it that and  people often say well you know maybe  tesla they've had one of the most  successful ipos of any company in this  space and certainly have gotten a lot of  press and mind share around the vehicles  they've created  and one of the things that i think is  notable it's not often talked about but  they did this with the support of three  major companies which could have looked  at tesla as a competitor but instead of  embrace the opportunity to work with  them learn from them  take advantage of the entrepreneurial  culture that they have  and of course tesla has gotten benefits  from these relationships as well  as in the previous examples i mentioned  each one of these companies panasonic  daimler and toyota are strategic  investors in tesla  they invested a key moment just before  the company went public so it gave  kind of endorsement or validation of the  company at a key moment when they were  just entering the public market but each  has developed different strategic  relationships as well around product  development  joint r d  commercial deployment and so you'll see  tesla working with toyota on their rav4  vehicle or you'll see them working with  panasonic on the development of new  battery technologies  and these are the kinds of strategic  partnerships that we think need to  happen more  and for which there needs to be a more  efficient marketplace so in many ways  what we're trying to do at broad scale  is take these kinds of relationships  that companies are forging uh you know  with the the most promising new startups  in the space and create a marketplace  for those kinds of partnerships that's  more efficient  faster and more dynamic so really  thinking about it in a clearinghouse  model imagine if there was one place you  could go  where you knew that you could get access  to  all the corporates that were interested  in investing in or partnering with  promising startups in this space and it  just today doesn't exist if you ask  entrepreneurs if you ask venture  capitalists they'll say it's a very ad  hoc  process  it really  increasingly depends on personal  relationships that folks have  and it's slow and it's inefficient we  think that we can create through the  kind of network approach that i  mentioned a more efficient process a  more valuable process for all involved  reducing  the time needed to identify and forge  those partnerships and ultimately  accelerating innovation and the impacts  that we're all looking for  in sum then  i would include by saying that scaling  up innovation more than anything  requires new modes of collaboration 