l you might be  affiliated with if any  there's a mic there as well  ryan  joint degree first a first year joint  degree student with school of management  in the school of forestry um i was just  wondering whether you anticipate  creating  partnerships between the growth  companies themselves is i i have the  feeling that  some  technologies will have synergistic  effects if if those those two companies  are combined or um  work in a network with the the larger  companies  do you anticipate that  absolutely it's a great insight and as  we've spent a good portion of the last  year talking to some of the smartest  venture capitalists entrepreneurs and  other kind of observers in the market  one of the pieces of feedback we've  gotten is there are too many companies  trying to do the exact same thing or  there are complementary companies that  have different solutions which would  benefit by working more closely together  or even merging i think we're going to  see more of that activity in the years  to come  and part of it is that the nature of the  venture capital model is that they want  to launch lots of different businesses  and see kind of who can get to the  finish line but in this market you need  more collaboration and less kind of  cutthroat competition so  i think a lot of these businesses are  figuring out ways to collaborate and  some of the exciting things you'll see  will come from actors you might think  are natural allies and some from  you know companies you would never have  expected would be working together  yeah in the back there  hey i'm jeff woodward i'm a second year  joint degree student between the school  management and school forestry  um traditionally in entrepreneurship  there's a little bit of hesitancy about  partnering with a strategic investor  early on  because there's concern that it kind of  limits acquisition opportunities from  another strategic investor or creates  other perverse incentives  you seem to be in some of these case