g  at increasingly now some parts of the  utility industry are getting nervous  about it they're starting to see load  that's being taken from them by dg  customers some are pushing back on net  mirroring laws  but that means to me that kind of where  there's those kinds of concerns there's  also opportunities for forward thinking  uh utilities and so dg solar is i think  an area of interest so is the whole  issue of you know smart grid and grid  modernization  where you know if we can figure out ways  for utilities to be actively involved in  that uh then they won't be  disintermediated by other players but  you know that's a real issue and concern  as well and so there are a lot of pain  points but also areas i think that bring  opportunity as a result of that and  we're going to have to be creative in  looking at the regulatory model and  where we play in that sector but i agree  with you  yeah  my name is ray and i'm a second year at  the school of forestry i'm interested in  learning about um how the relationship  between  let's say a strategic and a smaller  company changes as they scale up um i'm  interested in hearing your perspective  about how the kind of carrots that um  each of these players pursue the  benefits that they pursue  change  i can imagine that for waste management  they in a way must admire the business  model of  recycle bank and in a way want to  emulate it  and then as recycle bank uh scales up  through using uh waste management's  assets maybe it'll run into different  kinds of problems um so how did the how  did the you know benefits uh evolve  that's a good question i i you know  nobody knows exactly how these  partnerships will evolve um but it's  certainly conceivable that you know as  the smaller entity grows and if the  partnership you know gets close enough  i mean there was an acquisition  announced a few weeks ago  where  one of the innovative new companies in  the sharing economy the maybe the most  best  known one zipcar  agreed to be acquired by avis  and you know you kind of think to  yourself well that's interesting i  wonder why that happened and i think one  of the dynamics there was probably the  fact that uh  as zipcar started to impact the market  with this short-term rental model other  players in the market got into it as  well the big incumbents so hertz  launched an offering that looked a whole  lot like that where they were allowing  hourly rentals and doing it on campuses  and so on and i think ava's just sort of  decided you know they could partner with  zipcar or they could just acquire them  and they decided to acquire them so i  think you're going to see a lot more of  that where you know  companies are going to have to make  decisions as they partner and grow  together do they stay separate do they  acquire do they joint venture  but all of that should be ultimately in  the name of getting those new  innovations to market  yes  hi thanks for coming my name is will  george i'm a first year in the forestry  school  and i was wondering can you talk a  little bit about the portfolio of  companies that you have in terms of  business model innovation versus  technology innovation and  kind of  you know do you have a certain do you  like a certain  you know percentage of each or  do the companies that you work with  are they looking for  one more than the other right  so  i can't talk about any specific  companies yet because we haven't  announced any deals but we are working  with a number of companies on the growth  stage side and some of them are business  model innovations and some of them are  technology and i think it will continue  to be a fair split of those two  the advantage business model innovations  as i kind of referenced before is that  you don't have as much technology risk  and so they might be perfectly situated  to partner with a large corporate to get  more traction in the market  uh because they don't present a whole  lot of risk uh in terms of technology