ners and what kind of screening  criteria that you apply when you're  looking at these new opportunities sure  i mean we're using a lot of the same  criteria that any venture investor would  look at so obviously wanting to make  sure that there's a strong team  in place that they've got good  technology they've got defensible  ip or other unique assets  but because we're really looking at this  from the standpoint of how we can scale  up innovation we want to make sure that  the company has a solution or an  offering that's really market ready  so if they're in the early stage of kind  of working through the technology  development and testing and piloting it  it's probably not going to be ready yet  unless as we talked about earlier uh  it's a business model innovation in  which case it might get kind of  moved to the head of the  list in some ways because there's just  less technology risk there but by means  by by and large we're looking for  companies that have you know those good  fundamentals and are solving a challenge  or a problem that the large corporate  entities in our network are  are working on so it's i would say  that's maybe the other distinction is  we're going to be very driven  by the needs of our network and venture  capital generally is um  what's the right way to say it more  supply side focused right they come up  with they have a technology or company  that they identify they want to make  sure there's a good team and good  technology and then kind of only later  do they often say now who are we going  to sell this to  we're going to look at it from the  opposite direction what's the problem  that our network's trying to solve  and then who are the companies that can  solve that challenge  yes  from a first year graduate student from  school of forestry um you mentioned it  before i'm curious about as broad scale  move up move to other sectors how does  the strategy will change or the dynamics  of the partnerships will change and  do you foresee any challenges  well i think the you know as we talked  about before the dynamics in each of  these markets is a little different um  the way i think about it you know going  back to kind of one of the images i'll  just show it to you again as we talk  about it we really want to think about  this as one network one big distributed  network like paul buran talked about  with multiple nodes in the network so  power is a node waste water real estate  transportation  but the idea is that you can pull them  together  in  areas that are of common interest so  some of the challenges and issues that  you're all  familiar with take  electric vehicles electric vehicles  would be an issue for the power network  for the transportation or the power part  of the network the transportation  cluster in the network even the real  estate cluster because where you're  going to charge these things right  so increasingly the issues that are most  germane to  energy and environment are at the  intersection of these different  industries right take another issue like  waste to energy i mean obviously that  involves companies that are  waste collectors and processors as well  as companies that need energy inputs and  feedstocks so  a lot of these issues cross the  traditional boundaries of industries  right and that's the reason we want to  create this network of  companies from different industries  stuart  oh the  power industry is dealing with a number  of issues that you raise particularly  this lack of new technology that they  can actually use and deploy there's also  a huge  gap in terms of the people that are  actually going to help run these  industries do you ever view broad scale  as being a way to create a network of  new leaders within an industry and how  does that how would that actually  germinate come true because right now  they simply can't fill the gap of people  that are  that they need to work in these  industries and be the innovators that's  a great question um  and a natural coming from the head of  seabay  because it's true that these challenges  and industries will need new leaders  to figure out how these problems get  solved and i do think that you know as i  said earlier innovation is not just  about technology it's it's often about  the culture of the enterprise and that's  why we're trying to marry  entrepreneurial culture to  the assets that large organizations have  but it also means having the right  people who will think creatively and in  new ways about problem solving  and so i believe very much that there's  a need to get new folks into these kinds  of institutions and  too often  you go into meetings at some of these  companies and there's a  attitude like well we've never done that  this way in this industry so why should  we start doing it now  and the ceos who i talk to are  frustrated with that they actually would  like to see  people who are  risk takers and um who are trying to  drive change come into their enterprises  and they they try to hire them um but  you know it often it often is tough to  get to people to come into those  industries when they've got other  options available to them but i think  there are great opportunities for folks  from for example the cba program and som  and fds here  to go into these institutions to be  those kinds of catalysts of change and  to be the ones who can actually drive it  okay  well thank you all for your attention  your great questions i've really enjoyed  the dialogue and happy to talk to any of  you after thanks a lot

