ndustry's most  promising innovations  in 2000 andrew founded green order a  strategic advisory firm that worked with  more than a dozen a hundred leading  enterprises he has invested and advised  more than a dozen startups fortune  recently described him as green  businesses go-to guy  andrew is a graduate of brown university  and yale law school and has held  teaching appointments and research  fellowships at columbia harvard and yale  and he serves as i mentioned on the  advisory board of the yale center for  business the environment please join me  in welcoming welcoming andrew shapiro to  the 2012-2013  saban sustainable venture prize speaker  series  thank you stuart and thank you all for  braving the weather to get here  uh i'm really pleased to be back at yale  i've spent the day here today  and other than navigating my way through  eight foot mounds of snow on the  sidewalk it's it's been a real  a pleasure to be back um  you know it is kind of fitting that uh  we're having this discussion about new  strategies to innovate around energy and  resources at a time  when last night in the state of the  union  president obama spoke  i think at more length about climate  change and the need for energy  innovation than he has probably in any  state of the union  thus far in his presidency and also at a  time when you know we're just grappling  with the reality of extreme weather  events in our  uh everyday lives i mean the fact that  this area of connecticut had three feet  of snow  over this past weekend is really kind of  remarkable so i think it's a wake-up  call and a good time for us to have this  dialogue and conversation so i'm looking  forward to it i'm going to speak for  roughly 30 minutes  give you a little bit of perspective on  scaling up innovation as i think about  it and then i'm really looking forward  to your feedback and your questions and  a dialogue with all of you so i hope  you'll think about questions i know some  of you have already been giving thought  to questions you sent some my way that  were really great ones so i look forward  to the conversation with all of you  so a little bit more about my  perspective  i started a firm called green order in  2000  that worked with large companies and  works with large companies today  to help make energy and environmental  innovation a source of competitive  advantage and we've worked with a really  broad array of industries and corporate  leaders as stuart said  there's just a couple of examples here  on this slide but more than 100  companies across diverse industries  we were very fortunate to start working  with general electric in 2004  help them to create their business  strategy around clean technology called  eco-imagination some of you have  probably seen the marketing but  at its heart eco imagination was a  business strategy to invest in clean  technology and innovation around energy  and environment and so  i'll come back to the work that we've  done with ge it's really central to  where i've gone in my career over the  last seven or eight years  i also have had the occasion to work on  some other  fun and interesting projects we worked  with general motors on the launch of the  chevrolet volt  which was really a pioneering electric  vehicle  worked with silverstein properties in  new york on the creation of seven world  trade center the first building that  went up at ground zero which was the  first leed certified office tower in new  york city  and we worked with leading energy  companies like duke energy and national  grid and others  so i've had the benefit of working  across a variety of different sectors  and seeing how large corporates think  about  these issues  and the reason i'm excited  about this space is while there are  great challenges ahead of us when it  comes to energy security and  environmental issues and climate change  there's also great opportunity  uh and that opportunity i see is the  opportunity to solve our resource 