nt to improve their own  performance right they know that if they  can operate more efficiently they can  produce greater profitability more  enterprise value  they want to create products and  services that the market wants and by  innovating they hope to do that and  ultimately increase market share and get  price premiums and loyalty in the market  and they want better engagement with the  marketplace that means with public  stakeholders  but it also means  accessing innovation no matter where it  may be  and so companies like general electric  and others are embracing this concept of  open innovation and even though they  have huge resources and investments in  their own r d  general electric for example across  their global research centers has 3 000  scientists who do nothing but look at  new technologies and applications and  that's outside of their business units  where they have thousands of more  technologists and r d folks looking at  new ideas  so open innovation is this concept of  looking outside the four walls of the  enterprise whether it's investing in  university research or looking at  investing in new startups or working  collaboratively with others in the  marketplace  and this is a big economic opportunity  mckinsey put out a study last year  saying that over the next 20 years there  is three trillion dollars in economic  benefit by improving resource efficiency  in the global marketplace so there's a  lot at stake here and a lot of reason to  focus for big companies on this area  these are big markets  at the same time you've got some of the  most talented entrepreneurs and  investors who have gravitated into the  energy and resources area  because they see the same economic  opportunity and because they're driven  by ideals and values and they want to  make a difference in the world and  that's incredibly exciting to me  i started in this space in 2000  sort of at the height of the dot-com  bubble  and there were not that many people at  that