g  at increasingly now some parts of the  utility industry are getting nervous  about it they're starting to see load  that's being taken from them by dg  customers some are pushing back on net  mirroring laws  but that means to me that kind of where  there's those kinds of concerns there's  also opportunities for forward thinking  uh utilities and so dg solar is i think  an area of interest so is the whole  issue of you know smart grid and grid  modernization  where you know if we can figure out ways  for utilities to be actively involved in  that uh then they won't be  disintermediated by other players but  you know that's a real issue and concern  as well and so there are a lot of pain  points but also areas i think that bring  opportunity as a result of that and  we're going to have to be creative in  looking at the regulatory model and  where we play in that sector but i agree  with you  yeah  my name is ray and i'm a second year at  the school of forestry i'm interested in  learning about um how the relationship  between  let's say a strategic and a smaller  company changes as they scale up um i'm  interested in hearing your perspective  about how the kind of carrots that um  each of these players pursue the  benefits that they pursue  change  i can imagine that for waste management  they in a way must admire the business  model of  recycle bank and in a way want to  emulate it  and then as recycle bank uh scales up  through using uh waste management's  assets maybe it'll run into different  kinds of problems um so how did the how  did the you know benefits uh evolve  that's a good question i i you know  nobody knows exactly how these  partnerships will evolve um but it's  certainly conceivable that you know as  the smaller entity grows and if the  partnership you know gets close enough  i mean there was an acquisition  announced a few weeks ago  where  one of the innovative new companies in  the sharing economy the maybe the most  best  known one zipcar  agreed to be acquired by avis  and you know you kind of think to  yourself well that's interesting i  wonder why that happened and i think one  of the dynamics there was probably the  fact that uh  as zipcar started to impact the market  with this short-term rental model other  players in the market got into it as  well the big incumbents so hertz  launched an offering that looked a whole  lot like that where they were allowing  hourly rentals and doing it on campuses  and so on and i think ava's just sort of  decided you know they could partner with  zipcar or they could just acquire them  and they decided to acquire them so i  think you're going to see a lot more of  that where you know  companies are going to have to make  decisions as they partner and grow  together do they stay separate do they  acquire do they joint venture  but all of that should be ultimately in  the name of getting those new  innovations to market  yes  hi thanks for coming my name is will  george i'm a first year in the forestry  school  and i was wondering can you talk a  little bit about the portfolio of  companies that you have in terms of  business model innovation versus  technology innovation and  kind of  you know do you have a certain do you  like a certain  you know percentage of each or  do the companies that you work with  are they looking for  one more than the other right  so  i can't talk about any specific  companies yet because we haven't  announced any deals but we are working  with a number of companies on the growth  stage side and some of them are business  model innovations and some of them are  technology and i think it will continue  to be a fair split of those two  the advantage business model innovations  as i kind of referenced before is that  you don't have as much technology risk  and so they might be perfectly situated  to partner with a large corporate to get  more traction in the market  uh because they don't present a whole  lot of risk uh in terms of technology  actually the example i just mentioned  would be a good one right zipcar they're  not  recreating the vehicle they're not an  electric vehicle company they're not a  natural gas vehicle company they're not  you know even taking on the internal  combustion engine in any way  they are a business model innovation and  thus it was very easy for a larger  corporate like avis to  get involved and acquire them  because it wasn't you know making sure  that the technology worked for example  you know a lot of ev car companies out  there that have had some technological  challenges with the battery and so on so  that would be a good example  but i think we're going to continue to  see both i think business model  innovations often get overlooked  in the whole discussion about you know  how we innovate and create change and  and there can be powerful things  happening there i mean look at what's  happening in solar  where the manufacturers of solar have  had a very challenging time over the  last year but some of the great success  stories are coming from business model  innovations around leasing  ppas you know this whole no money down  model and that that's driving a huge  amount of adoption in the market  any other questions  yeah  i have another one um so i was wondering  about the scale of capital uh you know  because  again power sector obviously very  capital intensive  um  and sometimes this commercialization  value of death is is framed as a problem  of you know there's just doesn't seem to  be enough money to put at risk  considering that particular risk profile  it seems like  venture capital you have different  segments right vc you have pe like vc  wants to do  you know small investments you know  decent hefty amount of risk but you know  smaller investments pe wants to do  bigger investments but moderate to lower  kind of risk you know there doesn't seem  to be a good space for  large amount of money large risk which  seems to be where a lot of these  projects fall so i was kind of wondering  is there a gap there uh that the  just the epc  sector has just  hasn't seemed historically well equipped  to deal with so i think that's  absolutely right there's a there's a  kind of middle ground between  particularly between kind of late stage  venture and project finance where you've  got technologies that have been  tested and proven at pilot scale but  which have not gotten full commercial  deployment and thus have a difficult  time getting bank financing whether it's  equity or debt  um and so there's a there's a need to  finance those kinds of projects and i  think there are some new players who are  entering that market but you're right  the scale of capital needed to build  large facilities whether it's  manufacturing or  whether it's on the generation side or  certainly has been true in biofuels and  so on  can be  hundreds of millions of dollars  the other interesting thing though  that's happening is through these kinds  of partnerships you're seeing deals  where larger companies that have that  capability both from a balance sheet  perspective and a technical perspective  are partnering with the smaller  companies to sort of say outsource that  activity to us  i'll give you an example there's an led  company called bridge lux  that did a partnership with toshiba and  basically toshiba had all of this  excess fabricating  capacity from semiconductors that they  basically weren't using because of a  change in standards and they figured out  that they could take what was unused  manufacturing capacity and apply it to  this led technology and so the two  companies are working together and it's  a win-win because they've taken uh  something that was under utilized asset  and applied it in a new area and they've  licensed that technology and now they  have a play in that space so figuring  out those kinds of partnerships where  you know the larger player has the  capital to put to work but also the  technological know-how or an asset  that's underutilized makes a lot of  sense  uh we'll go there and then there  um i'm curious about why you chose the  power sector to start with and what else  you looked at or  yeah yeah  so  you know it's a good question we  also i think i didn't mention this but  part of our philosophy is that this all  has to be very relationship driven and  we have worked closely with those three  companies that i mentioned duke energy  general electric and national grid and  at the c-suite level and we believe that  having the buy-in of a senior executive  ideally the chief executive of the  company is key to getting the kind of  partnerships that we want to drive  because the just the time it can take  otherwise is  kind of  difficult to  to swallow so that was one reason we  started in that area  but we also canvassed a bunch of the  other verticals that i looked at or i  mentioned earlier  and there's interest from major players  in those sectors as well we just thought  we need to start somewhere and frankly  there's so much at stake in the power  sector i mean it's obviously from an  environmental standpoint uh the largest  impact that's out there  and there's also another dynamic which  is you know probably uh obvious but  that's the utilities don't generally  compete with one another because they're  regulated entities you can get multiple  utilities working side by side and even  though i've worked in other industries  like real estate and transportation and  waste management you know in those  sectors there's more cutthroat  competition so the idea of having  a gm or a ford or a toyota or a vw kind  of partner in an initial network cluster  seemed a little bit more challenging  than getting a duke in a national grid  who are two of the biggest utilities  in the western world  but they don't compete  so it was there was a strategic decision  there  could you talk a little bit about the  process between finding a growth company  and connecting it to  your partners and what kind of screening  criteria that you apply when you're  looking at these new opportunities sure  i mean we're using a lot of the same  criteria that any venture investor would  look at so obviously wanting to make  sure that there's a strong team  in place that they've got good  technology they've got defensible  ip or other unique assets  but because we're really looking at this  from the standpoint of how we can scale  up innovation we want to make sure that  the company has a solution or an  offering that's really market ready  so if they're in the early stage of kind  of working through the technology  development and testing and piloting it  it's probably not going to be ready yet  unless as we talked about earlier uh  it's a business model innovation in  which case it might get kind of  moved to the head of the  list in some ways because there's just  less technology risk there but by means  by by and large we're looking for  companies that have you know those good  fundamentals and are solving a challenge  or a problem that the large corporate  entities in our network are  are working on so it's i would say  that's maybe the other distinction is  we're going to be very driven  by the needs of our network and venture  capital generally is um  what's the right way to say it more  supply side focused right they come up  with they have a technology or company  that they identify they want to make  sure there's a good team and good  technology and then kind of only later  do they often say now who are we going  to sell this to  we're going to look at it from the  opposite direction what's the problem  that our network's trying to solve  and then who are the companies that can  solve that challenge  yes  from a first year graduate student from  school of forestry um you mentioned it  before i'm curious about as broad scale  move up move to other sectors how does  the strategy will change or the dynamics  of the partnerships will change and  do you foresee any challenges  well i think the you know as we talked  about before the dynamics in each of  these markets is a little different um  the way i think about it you know going  back to kind of one of the images i'll  just show it to you again as we talk  about it we really want to think about  this as one network one big distributed  network like paul buran talked about  with multiple nodes in the network so  power is a node waste water real estate  transportation  but the idea is that you can pull them  together  in  areas that are of common interest so  some of the challenges and issues that  you're all  familiar with take  electric vehicles electric vehicles  would be an issue for the power network  for the transportation or the power part  of the network the transportation  cluster in the network even the real  estate cluster because where you're  going to charge these things right  so increasingly the issues that are most  germane to  energy and environment are at the  intersection of these different  industries right take another issue like  waste to energy i mean obviously that  involves companies that are  waste collectors and processors as well  as companies that need energy inputs and  feedstocks so  a lot of these issues cross the  traditional boundaries of industries  right and that's the reason we want to  create this network of  companies from different industries  stuart  oh the  power industry is dealing with a number  of issues that you raise particularly  this lack of new technology that they  can actually use and deploy there's also  a huge  gap in terms of the people that are  actually going to help run these  industries do you ever view broad scale  as being a way to create a network of  new leaders within an industry and how  does that how would that actually  germinate come true because right now  they simply can't fill the gap of people  that are  that they need to work in these  industries and be the innovators that's  a great question um  and a natural coming from the head of  seabay  because it's true that these challenges  and industries will need new leaders  to figure out how these problems get  solved and i do think that you know as i  said earlier innovation is not just  about technology it's it's often about  the culture of the enterprise and that's  why we're trying to marry  entrepreneurial culture to  the assets that large organizations have  but it also means having the right  people who will think creatively and in  new ways about problem solving  and so i believe very much that there's  a need to get new folks into these kinds  of institutions and  too often  you go into meetings at some of these  companies and there's a  attitude like well we've never done that  this way in this industry so why should  we start doing it now  and the ceos who i talk to are  frustrated with that they actually would  like to see  people who are  risk takers and um who are trying to  drive change come into their enterprises  and they they try to hire them um but  you know it often it often is tough to  get to people to come into those  industries when they've got other  options available to them but i think  there are great opportunities for folks  from for example the cba program and som  and fds here  to go into these institutions to be  those kinds of catalysts of change and  to be the ones who can actually drive it  okay  well thank you all for your attention  your great questions i've really enjoyed  the dialogue and happy to talk to any of  you after thanks a lot
 

